WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
INTRO:
The first companies that come to mind when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:08.560
you think about EVs are Tesla, Rivian, Nio, and&nbsp;
Lucid. In terms of legacy automakers, Volkswagen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:14.080
and Ford are making a pretty good push as well.&nbsp;
But, have you ever heard of a company called BYD?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.440
In the western world, BYD vehicles are basically&nbsp;
non-existent, but in China, BYD is a rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.440
popular choice. BYD isn’t some new startup that&nbsp;
was created to leverage EV hype either. BYD has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.720
actually been around since 1995, and they’ve been&nbsp;
involved in the automotive business since 2003.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.880
Moreover, unlike Lucid and Rivian&nbsp;
who have barely sold any cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:43.680
BYD is a leader in EV sales. In fact, within the&nbsp;
first 9 months of 2021, BYD was only beaten out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.720
by Tesla in terms of EV deliveries, and they’re&nbsp;
currently the third largest automotive company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.240
in the world by market cap. BYD was actually&nbsp;
the world's largest EV company until Tesla’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:00.000
recent parabolic run. So, here’s the story of the&nbsp;
EV behemoth that most of you have never heard of.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.200
FOUNDING BYD:&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.200
Taking a look back, the story of BYD dates back&nbsp;
to a poor man named Wang Chuanfu. Wang was born on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:17.360
April 8, 1966 in Wuwei County to a couple of poor&nbsp;
farmers. By the time Wang was a teenager, both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.320
of his parents had passed away, and he was being&nbsp;
raised by his older brother and sister. For Wang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.200
education was the only viable path out of his&nbsp;
situation, so he attended Central South University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.760
where he majored in chemistry. He would instantly&nbsp;
turn around and get his masters from the Beijing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.800
Non-Ferrous research institute in 1990. Wang’s&nbsp;
initial goal was to simply live a modest life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.160
by being a good engineer, and as such, Wang became&nbsp;
a government researcher out of college. He worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.160
at the General Research Institute for Nonferrous&nbsp;
Metals where his job was to research rare earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.480
metals that could be used in batteries. He spent&nbsp;
a couple of years here, but he found very little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.480
fulfillment from this job. So, in 1995, he teamed&nbsp;
up with his cousin, Lu Xiangyang, to create BYD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.600
Wang has never revealed what the true meaning&nbsp;
of BYD is, but he often jokes that it stands for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.600
Bring You Dollars or Build Your Dreams. Anyway,&nbsp;
BYD wasn’t an automotive company from day one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.720
they were actually a battery company. Wang didn’t&nbsp;
have any connections or very much brand power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.280
but what he did have was expertise in batteries.&nbsp;
And given that the laptop and mobile phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.840
markets were booming, this knowledge was&nbsp;
extremely valuable. BYD started off by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.000
producing batteries for small consumer tech&nbsp;
items like portable lights and solar modules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.160
But, as BYD built a name for themselves, they&nbsp;
were able to secure larger jobs like producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.120
batteries for Black &amp; Decker power tools, Nokia&nbsp;
handsets, Dell laptops, and most importantly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.480
classic Motorola Razr flip phone. Wang was able&nbsp;
to build a great relationship with Motorola.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.120
In fact, Motorola was so impressed with BYD&nbsp;
that the chairman of Motorola awarded BYD for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.080
being an excellent supplier. The road wasn’t&nbsp;
all smooth though. Two notable companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.240
Sanyo Electric Co. and Sony, would sue&nbsp;
BYD for intellectual property theft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.200
BYD would end up settling with Sanyo while the&nbsp;
Sony lawsuit was rejected by a Japanese court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.200
If you were being sued by these guys, it&nbsp;
definitely meant that you were a serious threat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.200
So, now that BYD had built a name for themselves,&nbsp;
Wang decided to take the company public in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.000
2002. And using the raised funds, Wang purchased&nbsp;
a majority stake in a failing state owned car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
company called Xi’an Qinchuan Auto Company,&nbsp;
and this marks the beginning of BYD auto.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.840
BYD AUTO:&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.120
At first, BYD simply continued to sell the&nbsp;
Flyer that Xi’an had already developed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.320
The Flyer sold decently pulling in 20,000 sales&nbsp;
per year, but it wasn’t anything too crazy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.400
In the background though, BYD was working hard at&nbsp;
reverse engineering every car they could get their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:40.480
hands on. They reverse engineered engines, molds,&nbsp;
motors, entertainment systems, and Wang’s S class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.960
In other words, they were copying everything.&nbsp;
Wang was actually notorious for ordering large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.880
quantities of parts from a notable supplier,&nbsp;
and then never ordering again because he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.120
reverse engineered the item and learnt to build&nbsp;
it himself. After a few years of copying, I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.160
reverse engineering, BYD came out with their&nbsp;
first model since the acquisition called the F3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.280
The front of the F3 looks awfully similar to the&nbsp;
2003 Toyota Corolla. But despite their similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.080
looks, the F3 was far cheaper which made it much&nbsp;
more attractive to many customers. Over the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.120
few years, BYD launched a handful of cars like&nbsp;
the F6, and basically all of these were ripoffs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.440
different vehicles from Toyota. It should also be&nbsp;
noted that none of these vehicles were electric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.760
so BYD wasn’t really adding anything to&nbsp;
these vehicles other than a lower price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.480
It wasn’t until 2008 that BYD jumped into the&nbsp;
EV market with the launch of the F3DM. The F3DM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.200
was a dual motor hybrid that had a pure electric&nbsp;
range of 60 miles, and it was actually the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.680
plug-in hybrid in the world. But despite this&nbsp;
title, consumers didn’t really care. The F3DM was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:44.960
basically just an electric corolla, and it didn’t&nbsp;
have the appeal that EVs have today. So, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.160
launch ended up being a massive flop in both China&nbsp;
and internationally. But despite the underwhelming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.440
launch, BYD was definitely onto something. They&nbsp;
were targeting the utilitarian EV market which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.440
is likely to become the biggest portion of&nbsp;
the EV industry over the next few decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.640
But, in the late 2000s, this market simply wasn’t&nbsp;
developed yet. Most utility car buyers preferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.280
to stick with cheaper gas alternatives, and EV&nbsp;
customers were generally premium consumers. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.400
BYD was stuck in no man’s land. Wang didn’t want&nbsp;
to jump into the luxury vehicle market though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.760
so he looked closer into who might be interested&nbsp;
in utility focused EVs and the answer was clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.320
governments. Governments don’t care about&nbsp;
buying cool looking vehicles. They just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.320
want the most cost effective vehicle and&nbsp;
if it's eco friendly, that’s just a bonus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.920
So, in 2009, BYD started focusing their&nbsp;
efforts into mass producing electric buses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.840
BYD sold these buses to Amsterdam,&nbsp;
Frankfurt, Los Angeles, and Albuquerque.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.440
But, this launch was actually quite controversial.&nbsp;
Albuquerque would end up returning the buses due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.600
to mechanical issues. Meanwhile, California&nbsp;
received several complaints that the busses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:53.360
were manufactured by people earning just $1.50&nbsp;
per hour. Despite these concerns, BYD continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.880
to target the government by manufacturing&nbsp;
electric garbage trucks, airport shuttles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:02.240
tractors, and mining trucks. Around this same&nbsp;
time, Warren Buffett would invest $232 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.200
into BYD in 2008 which attracted international&nbsp;
interest into the company. All of this extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.480
interest combined with their government endeavors&nbsp;
allowed BYD to reach 500,000 deliveries by 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.880
To put that in perspective, Tesla didn’t reach&nbsp;
that figure until 10 years later in 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.640
But unlike Tesla who is blasting past that number,&nbsp;
BYD has been stuck at half a million for a decade.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.320
REBRANDING:&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.240
While a decade of no sales growth sounds&nbsp;
really bad from the surface, it’s actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.520
not too bad in BYD’s case. Wang figured that&nbsp;
the utility consumer EV market still had years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:40.400
if not decades to develop. I mean even now, sub&nbsp;
$30,000 EVs aren’t that popular. So, Wang decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.640
to simply maintain what BYD was doing and instead&nbsp;
focus their effort into building a real brand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.080
Up until then, BYD was really just a Chinese&nbsp;
knockoff that people only bought for the price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.880
But, this would all change with BYD’s second&nbsp;
generation cars. The first of these second gen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.480
vehicles was the BYD Qin hybrid which debuted in&nbsp;
2013. Just by looking at it, you can see that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:05.520
was nothing like BYD’s older vehicles. The Qin&nbsp;
wasn’t a super sleek vehicle or anything, but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:11.120
the first time, it was original. Aside from its&nbsp;
unique look, the Qin was also quite affordable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.560
The Qin wasn’t able to live up to BYD’s&nbsp;
promised launch price of just under $30,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.040
But it did launch for $35,000 which is still&nbsp;
cheaper than the model 3 today. Following the Qin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.640
BYD came out with the Yuan in March of 2016. The&nbsp;
Yuan was offered as a compact crossover SUV that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.080
was offered as a hybrid and as a full EV. The&nbsp;
hybrid version cost between $32,000 and $38,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.800
so still under that $40k mark. Both the Qin and&nbsp;
Yuan were solid vehicles, but by far their biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.600
jump forward came with the Han which was released&nbsp;
in July of 2020. The Han instantly sticks out as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.640
a modern EV with its sleek design. But not only&nbsp;
does it look good, it’s also quite affordable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.880
The standard range comes with 314 miles of range&nbsp;
and only costs $33,730. You can also get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:01.600
extended range which comes with 376 miles of range&nbsp;
and costs $36,540. BYD quickly followed up the Han&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.160
with a sleek SUV called the Song in September&nbsp;
of 2020. The Song also boasts a range above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:11.760
300 miles and comes in under $40,000. So, though&nbsp;
BYD’s sales have remained stagnant for a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:16.080
I think we’d agree that BYD is a completely&nbsp;
different company today than they were in 2010.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.840
BYD TODAY:&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:25.360
As for BYD today, I believe that they’re on the&nbsp;
cusp of a massive boom. In July, the Han crossed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.360
100,000 deliveries, and I don’t really see&nbsp;
anything stopping them from reaching millions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.040
Also, BYD formed a partnership with Toyota two&nbsp;
years ago. Originally, it seemed like they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.040
just going to manufacture some models together,&nbsp;
but they actually ended up creating a new car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.320
company BYD Toyota in April of 2020. It’s not&nbsp;
clear when they will launch their first car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.840
but it should be within the next few years as&nbsp;
neither of them are new to the car business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.480
In terms of international expansion, BYD&nbsp;
does have some presence within Latin American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:56.800
and some Scandinavian countries, but the vast&nbsp;
majority of their business comes from China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.520
Wang does eventually want to spend more time on&nbsp;
the western market with his long term goal being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:06.560
20-30% of sales coming from outside China. But,&nbsp;
even if that doesn’t materialize, it’s not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.760
big of a deal given the strength of the Chinese EV&nbsp;
market. The CCP has made some super questionable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:16.720
moves in the past, but their embracement of&nbsp;
EVs is definitely not one of them. Experts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:22.000
predict that as much as 40% of Chinese vehicles&nbsp;
could be electric by 2030. Considering this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.600
it wouldn’t be that surprising to see BYD overtake&nbsp;
Toyota within this decade, but it is extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:33.520
ironic. BYD went from knocking off Toyota, to&nbsp;
partnering with Toyota, to overtaking Toyota.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.240
As for Wang, his net worth comes in at about $25&nbsp;
billion which puts him comfortably within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:43.360
top 100 richest people in the world. And I think&nbsp;
Wang more than deserves all of it given that BYD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:48.880
is one of the only companies in the EV space that&nbsp;
not only has hype but also results. Were you guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:54.080
familiar with BYD? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you think that legacy automakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:58.560
should get more serious about EVs. And of course,&nbsp;
consider joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.080
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
